{
  "step1_summary": "- Chief complaint: Acute breathing difficulty after a wasp sting (~15 minutes prior).\n- Symptoms: Onset ~15 minutes after wasp sting while gardening. Shallow breathing, chest tightness, tingling of the lips, blotchy rash. No reported throat closing or obvious lip swelling. Severity sufficient for patient/doctor concern; clinician advised ambulance. Aggravating factor: recent wasp sting; no clear alleviating measures attempted except availability of antihistamine. Patient has antihistamines at home.\n- Past medical history: Eczema. \"Aerosinusitis\" mentioned (used antihistamines for flying). No other significant PMH reported.\n- Medications/allergies: No regular medications reported. Allergic to penicillin. Has antihistamines at home.\n- Family/social history: Not discussed beyond wife present at home to call ambulance.\n- Vital signs/exam findings: Not recorded in transcript; clinician asked about swelling, wheeze; patient reports no throat swelling, some lip tingling, blotchy rash.\n- Labs/imaging: None mentioned.",
  "step2_vindicate": {
    "V": [
      "Anaphylactic circulatory collapse (hypotension from vasodilation): Vasodilation and hypotension are features of severe allergic reactions following stings; considered because of acute onset after sting and respiratory symptoms although no BP recorded."
    ],
    "I": [
      "Venom-induced inflammatory reaction: Local/systemic inflammatory response to insect venom causing urticaria and bronchospasm; fits timing after sting."
    ],
    "N": [
      "Mast cell/MC neoplasm unmasked by sting (e.g., systemic mastocytosis): Rarely, insect stings can trigger exaggerated systemic reactions in underlying mast cell disorders; less likely but possible given severe reaction to sting."
    ],
    "D": [
      "None likely relevant (degenerative/deficiency processes do not explain acute post-sting presentation)."
    ],
    "I2": [
      "Venom toxin-mediated systemic toxicity: Certain Hymenoptera venoms can cause direct toxic effects (e.g., neurotoxic or vasoactive), particularly after multiple stings; possible mechanism of acute symptoms after sting."
    ],
    "C": [
      "Hereditary angioedema (HAE): Presents with swelling after triggers (trauma, stress); usually without urticaria but can cause airway compromise. Unlikely but in differential for acute post-trauma angioedema."
    ],
    "A": [
      "IgE-mediated anaphylaxis (systemic allergic reaction) to wasp venom: Strongly suggested by rapid onset after sting, respiratory symptoms (breathlessness/tight chest), lip tingling (perioral paresthesia), and rash (blotching/urticaria).",
      "Large local allergic reaction / cutaneous hypersensitivity: Localized swelling and erythema at sting site and surrounding blotchy rash without systemic collapse."
    ],
    "T": [
      "Local traumatic reaction to sting (pain, localized swelling, erythema): Expected immediate local response to insect sting; could contribute to symptoms but does not explain systemic breathlessness alone."
    ],
    "E": [
      "Anxiety-induced hyperventilation or panic attack triggered by sting event: Acute onset of shallow breathing and chest tightness can be anxiety-related after a stressful event; rash and lip tingling make primary anxiety less likely but still possible."
    ]
  },
